{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Acute upper lip swelling after eating prawn soup, now with breathing difficulty and dizziness.\n- Symptoms:\n  - Onset: ~30 minutes after eating prawn soup around 19:00.\n  - Course: Progressive swelling of upper lip (started at left corner), sensation of \"pumping\"/getting bigger.\n  - Associated: Throat tightness/feeling of airway closing, difficulty breathing, possible chest tightness, dizziness.\n  - No specific mention of rash/hives, wheeze, vomiting, abdominal pain, or syncope.\n  - Aggravating/alleviating factors: Triggered after prawn ingestion; no meds taken prior to call.\n- Past medical history: Asthma; known peanut allergy (severe in childhood requiring injection and hospital visit). Prior similar episode about a year ago after late-night Chinese food with swelling and breathing difficulty.\n- Medications/allergies:\n  - Meds: Salbutamol inhaler; no maintenance (brown) inhaler; occasional unspecified antihistamines.\n  - Allergies: Peanut allergy; probable new shellfish (prawn) allergy.\n- Family/social history: Drinks alcohol socially (episode a year ago occurred after drinking); currently at home with mother available; no family history of angioedema noted.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: No vitals taken (telehealth). Subjective worsening lip swelling, dyspnea, and dizziness; no visible exam findings documented.\n- Labs/imaging: None.\n- Disposition in call: Doctor concerned for anaphylaxis; advised antihistamine while awaiting ambulance; instructed to call emergency services.",
